# Logitech G213 Prodigy (046d:c336), cabled, its HID++ interface 01 ->
# uaccess for the seat user. Not yet checked on this hardware: the id is
# from Solaar's listing of the keyboard and G HUB's device database, and the
# interface from OpenRGB's detector for it (interface 1, usage page 0xFF43)
# and a published list of its two hidraw nodes (Solaar issue 2665), read
# 2026-10-21. The keyboard has no receiver, so its USB identity is its own
# and the property match below is scoped by interface, as the G915's is.
#
# This node is taken to carry what is typed on the G213 beside HID++, as
# the G815's interface 01 does: a keyboard with two interfaces puts HID++
# beside its own reports. gearbench-ctl report shows which reports the
# node's descriptor holds, read from sysfs. So a program the seat's user
# runs can read what is typed on the keyboard while this rule is installed;
# Gearbench drops those reports unread. Granted through uaccess, as the
# G815's interface 01 is (70-g815.rules). Interface 00, the boot keyboard,
# gets nothing.
#
# The grant crosses accounts, as 70-gearbench-uinput.rules says of
# /dev/uinput's: a program that opened the node keeps reading it after another
# account gets the seat, and so reads what that account types on the G213, a
# password at the login screen included. On a machine several people use, that
# file's group grant, in a copy of this file in /etc/udev/rules.d/, limits the
# node to the group's accounts. A member then reads it from any session,
# whoever has the seat.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c336", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="01", TAG+="uaccess"
